# Airness Payload Layer Guide

This guide applies to every module that inherits `airness-parent-payload`. It adds to the generic guide and to
the node rules; it never relaxes them. A Payload module is a node project whose `package.json` declares
`payload` and `@airness/payload`, built with `pom` packaging.

## Verification

The Payload gates run through `airness:node-gate` like every node gate, bound by `airness-parent-payload`:

| Gate | Phase | What it checks |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `preflight` | `process-sources` | The node project holds a Payload application, and every Payload application declares `payload`, `graphql`, `sharp`, `next`, `react` and `react-dom` at the versions the layer pins; every `@payloadcms/*` package is at the Payload version. |
| `payload-blocklist` | `post-integration-test` | The lockfile resolves no package the Payload layer refuses: the MongoDB adapter, and the drivers of database servers Payload has no adapter for. |
| `payload-source-rules` | `post-integration-test` | Local API calls state their bounds, thread `req`, and decide access; every handler an `endpoints:` entry serves comes from `src/endpoints`; every collection and global decides its access; auth collections lock accounts; uploads are restricted; a configuration that pushes no schema records its migrations; nothing writes at boot. |
| `admin-views` | `post-integration-test` | Every custom admin view has an accessibility specification of the project's own. |
| `payload-environment` | `post-integration-test` | Every variable `src/lib/environment.ts` reads is documented in the repository's example file, and every `NEXT_PUBLIC_` variable it inlines is declared by an `ARG` in each Dockerfile that builds the member. |
| `payload-generated` | `post-integration-test` | Regenerating the Payload types, the admin import map and, when the member keeps `src/payload-generated-schema.ts`, the database schema with the project's own Payload CLI leaves them unchanged. |
| `payload-defaults` | `post-integration-test` | Every collection and global of the built configuration, framework-built ones included, decides its access; no drafts read serves an unapproved document to a stranger; production authentication limits are valid; `admin.autoLogin` is off. |
| `payload-migrations` | `post-integration-test` | The schema the configuration builds matches the newest `.json` snapshot the committed migrations record, compared as `payload migrate:create` compares it, without a database and without writing a file. |

Run one gate with `pnpm exec airness-payload gate <id> --verbose` to diagnose it. That is a diagnosis, never a verdict.

The node gates apply the Payload layer's additions too. The `eslint` gate runs the node rules plus the Payload
blocks below, and the Extended `e2e` gate runs the layer's `access-boundary` specification, which asks Payload's
`/api/access` what an anonymous caller may do and fails on every grant `publicAccess` does not declare. The
browser sweep scans a custom admin view within Payload's template: the layer supplies the template's content
selectors through `airness.layer.browser`, so Payload's own navigation and header are not judged as the view's.

## Settings

The Payload settings live under the `airnessPayload` key of the member's `package.json`; the node settings stay
under `airness`.

| Setting | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `publicAccess` | empty | Entries with `entity`, `operation`, `reason` and optional `fields`, declaring the anonymous permissions the project grants on purpose. An entry missing a field is dropped. |
| `analysisEnv` | placeholders for `PAYLOAD_SECRET` and `DATABASE_URL` | Variables the gates that import the configuration use when neither the process nor `.env.local`/`.env` supplies them. They let the configuration evaluate; nothing connects to them. |

The layer contributes defaults to the node settings of every member that declares `@airness/payload`: the generated
files (`src/payload-types.ts`, `src/payload-generated-schema.ts`, everything under `src/app/(payload)`, the
admin and API glue Payload scaffolds as generated, and the `src/migrations/*.json` schema snapshots), the framework
glue (`src/payload.config.ts`), the pure-logic floor (`src/access`, `src/lib`) and the routes the accessibility
sweep leaves to Payload (`/admin`, `/api`). Agents may not edit the generated files; regenerate them with the
Payload CLI. The installed layer contributes exact security replacements for
affected Payload dependency edges through `airness.layer.securityOverrides`; a project may use only those approvals
and the generic Airness approvals.

## Application Code

- Keep environment reads in `src/lib/environment.ts`, validate them there, and document each variable in the
  example file at the repository root.
- Collection, global, field and block definitions are declarative. Define access rules and pure logic in
  `src/access` or `src/lib`, hooks in `src/hooks`, and import them by reference.
- A collection decides `create`, `read`, `update` and `delete`; a global decides `read` and `update`.
- At a Local API call use an explicit options object. A read states its `depth` or `limit`; a call through
  `req.payload` or `request.payload` passes `req`; a call carrying `user`, a call composed with a spread, and
  every call in an endpoint module, a route handler or a server action (a `'use server'` module or function),
  or in a function of the project one of them calls, states `overrideAccess: false` after any spread. A name
  bound to `await getPayload(...)` is a Local API receiver like `payload`.
- Write every handler a collection, global or root `endpoints:` entry serves in a module under `src/endpoints`,
  and import it there by a relative or `@/` path, so the endpoint rule above judges its calls. A handler written
  in place, or imported from `src/lib`, `src/hooks` or another alias, is refused.
- A required relationship or upload, on a collection or a global, needs a `beforeDelete` hook on its target
  collection that removes the dependants first, unless drafts are enabled on the holder.
- A database adapter that does not push its schema needs a recorded migration for every schema change: after a
  change, run `payload migrate:create <name>` and commit the migration with the `.json` snapshot it writes.
- ESLint refuses an inline function in a collection, global, field or block configuration, a read of
  `process.env` outside `src/lib/environment.ts` and `src/proxy.ts`, and `payload` destructured out of `req`
  or `request` (call `req.payload` and pass `req`). A hook writes request state only on `req.context`. A migration keeps the
  snake-case name `payload migrate:create` gives it.

